The premises are mathematical: they posit structures and assert nothing about the world. Unlike the primer, which is expository and claims nothing new, the note's arrangement of the steps is the series' own, although each ingredient is classical and is attributed where it is used. Nothing in the primer, and nothing in the toolkit T1 imports from it, depends on this note.

Let $(W,B)$ be as in the Setup: radical of dimension $r \geq 1$, nondegenerate part of signature $(p,q)$, $m = \dim W = p + q + r$. Then $(W,B)$ admits a completion, and:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every completion satisfies $\dim \widetilde W \geq m + r$;
\item completions of dimension exactly $m + r$ exist, and every such has signature $(p{+}r,\, q{+}r)$;
\item any two minimal completions are isometric by an isometry fixing $W$ pointwise.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently $\Cl(p,q,r)$ embeds in $\Cl(p{+}r, q{+}r)$, minimally so among the embeddings induced by completions (no completion of dimension below $m + r$ exists), and the minimal ambient algebra is unique up to an isomorphism fixing the image of $\Cl(p,q,r)$.

Let $V = W' \oplus W$ with Witt basis $v_i \in W'$, $w_i \in W$ ($1 \le i \le k$) as in \claimref{std-algebra.witt-basis-car}, so $V \cong \R^{k,k}$. On $\Lambda W$ let $w \in W$ act by $w \wedge{}$ and $v \in W'$ by the contraction $\iota_{2B(v, \cdot)}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] this extends to an algebra isomorphism $\Cl(V, Q) \cong \End(\Lambda W) \cong \Mat_{2^{k}}(\R)$;
\item[(ii)] $f = v_1 w_1 v_2 w_2 \cdots v_k w_k$ is a primitive idempotent with $v_i f = 0$ for all $i$; the left ideal $\Cl(V, Q)\, f$ is a spinor space of dimension $2^{k}$ with basis $\{w_I f\}$, and $w_I f \mapsto w_{i_1} \wedge \dots \wedge w_{i_m}$ is an isomorphism of $\Cl(V, Q)$-modules $\Cl(V, Q)\, f \cong \Lambda W$.
\end{enumerate}
So the Fock space is the minimal left ideal generated by the vacuum idempotent $f$, and the $w_I f$ are its occupation-number basis.

Let $A$ be an associative unital real algebra containing elements $E_{ij}$, $1 \le i, j \le m$, with
\begin{equation}
E_{ij} E_{kl} = \delta_{jk} E_{il}, \qquad \textstyle\sum_{i} E_{ii} = 1 .
\end{equation}
Then $C = E_{11} A E_{11}$ is a unital algebra (with unit $E_{11}$) and
\begin{equation}
\Phi : A \to \Mat_m(C), \qquad \Phi(b)_{ij} = E_{1i}\, b\, E_{j1},
\end{equation}
is an isomorphism of unital algebras with inverse $(c_{ij}) \mapsto \sum_{i,j} E_{i1} c_{ij} E_{1j}$. In $\Cl(1,1)$, with $f = e_1 e_2$ ($f^{2} = 1$) and $\varepsilon_{\pm} = \tfrac12(1 \pm f)$, the elements $E_{11} = \varepsilon_+$, $E_{12} = \varepsilon_+ e_1$, $E_{21} = \varepsilon_- e_1$, $E_{22} = \varepsilon_-$ are such a system with $C = \R$, so $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$; in $\Cl(1,1,1)$ the same system has a two-dimensional corner $C \cong \Cl(0,0,1)$, so $\Cl(1,1,1) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl(0,0,1))$.

Let $v_1, \dots, v_k, w_1, \dots, w_k \in V$ be a \emph{Witt basis} of their span: $B(v_i, v_j) = B(w_i, w_j) = 0$ and $B(v_i, w_j) = \tfrac12 \delta_{ij}$. Then in $\Cl(V, Q)$
\begin{equation}
v_i v_j + v_j v_i = 0, \qquad w_i w_j + w_j w_i = 0, \qquad v_i w_j + w_j v_i = \delta_{ij},
\end{equation}
the canonical anticommutation relations for $k$ modes (read $v_i$ as annihilators, $w_i$ as creators). The elements $N_i = w_i v_i$ are mutually commuting idempotents with $N_i + v_i w_i = 1$. In particular, for orthogonal hyperbolic planes $\operatorname{span}(e_i, e_{k+i})$ of $\R^{k,k}$, $v_i = \tfrac12(e_i + e_{k+i})$ and $w_i = \tfrac12(e_i - e_{k+i})$ form such a basis. The form need not be nondegenerate outside the span.
